  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">RESTORING &amp; MANAGING HABITAT IN JAMAICA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Blue and John Crow Mountains National Park is an area of exceptional importance for migratory birds and has been globally recognized as a United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization (UNESCO) World Heritage Site, an Important Bird Area, a Key Biodiversity Area, an Alliance for Zero Extinction site, and a Center of Plant Diversity. It is managed by the non-profit organization Jamaica Conservation and Development Trust (JCDT) in partnership with the government. While the park serves as essential wintering grounds for many Neotropical migratory birds, this project focuses on improving habitat for Prairie Warbler, a species for which tropical deforestation is the primary threat. Partners will build upon the accomplishments of Phase I - including successful reforestation, community engagement, expanded revenue generation by JCDT and the initiation of a bird monitoring program - by scaling up reforestation, addressing the causes of park encroachment and deforestation, expanding educational outreach, and refining bird monitoring. American Bird Conservancy and JCDT will work in collaboration with local communities to 1) plant 12,000 native trees to restore over 40 to 50 acres of tropical forest; 2) mark 7 miles of park boundaries using native Blue Mahoe trees; 3) implement an innovative mentorship program to address the root causes of encroachment and subsequent deforestation; 4) provide targeted environmental outreach to local communities; and 5) expand a bird monitoring program to better survey Neotropical migratory birds and to understand their use of restored habitats and recently burned areas.</narrative>
  </description>
